  "query_preview": ".../Friday through Monday/...As the low dissipates \nover the southern Gulf and shifts east, high pressure builds over \nSEAK, bringing dry and mild conditions, lighter outflow winds and \nmostly sunny skies for Friday. The only exception could be along \nthe central and northeast Gulf coast as a weak boundary lifts \ncreeps northeast. High temps will be in the mid to upper 50s, with\na few low 60s possible for POW Island. A pleasant end to the week\nbefore the next fronts impact the region this weekend. The big \nplayer that will send the fronts into the Gulf and through SEAK \nwill be the remnants of Typhoon Dujuan and associated upper-level \nlow/trough that will slowly swing through southern interior into \nthe Gulf through the weekend. Multiple fronts/lows will pivot \naround the southern periphery of the upper trough through the Gulf\nand into the panhandle over the weekend. The initial front will \nlift northeast through the Gulf into the panhandle late Friday \nnight into Saturday, with another quickly on its heals pushing \neast through the Gulf into the panhandle on Sunday. Multiple \nrounds of moderate to heavy rain and gusty winds are expected with\neach frontal passage, especially with the Sunday system it will \ntap into deeper feed of moisture out ahead of it. Additionally, a \nfew thunderstorms will be possible with cooler temps aloft with \nthe upper trough approaching resulting in some modest instability.\nFor early next week, model guidance begins to diverge quite a \nbit, but the general trend is for the active pattern to continue \nwith the upper trough/low persisting near to west of the area, \nwith another front/low lifting northeast somewhere into the Gulf \nand into the panhandle, bringing another round of gusty winds and \nmoderate to heavy rain. Some guidance is quite aggressive with \nthis low and bringing gales into the Gulf, but confidence on \nlocation and timing of this system is quite low given the model \nspread.",
